canonical_claim.statement:
Counterexample to the printed sentence (transcribed in P061-T22) when it is read with the general exposure mappings that the paper allows. For every integer $N\ge1$ let $\mathcal Z=\{0,1,2\}^N$, $\Pr[\mathbf Z=\mathbf z]=3^{-N}$ for every $\mathbf z\in\mathcal Z$ (the coordinates $Z_1,\ldots,Z_N$ are independent and uniform on $\{0,1,2\}$), $g_i(\mathbf z)=\mathbb 1\{z_i>0\}$ (the exposure mapping of Section 6.2, p. 11) and $y_i(\mathbf z)=z_i$. Then, for every $N\ge1$ and every unit $i$:
(a) the exposures $D_1,\ldots,D_N$ are mutually independent, the raw potential outcomes are bounded ($0\le y_i(\mathbf z)\le2$), and there is no interference between units in the sense of p. 12 ($y_i(\mathbf z)=y_i(\mathbf z')$ if $z_i=z_i'$);
(b) $\pi_i(1)=\tfrac23$ and $\pi_i(0)=\tfrac13$, so Condition 4.1 holds for $d=1$ and $d'=0$; and $\Pr[Z_i=1\mid D_i=1]=\Pr[Z_i=2\mid D_i=1]=\tfrac12$;
(c) $\overline y_i(1)=\tfrac32$ and $\overline y_i(0)=0$, so $\tau_i(1,0)=\tfrac32$ and the AEED is $\tau(1,0)=\tfrac32$;
(d) reading $d=1$, $d'=0$ in the displayed ‘average direct effect’ as raw assignment coordinates, every substituted vector lies in $\mathcal Z$ and, on every draw, $y_i(1;\mathbf D_{-i})-y_i(0;\mathbf D_{-i})=1$ and $y_i(1;\mathbf Z_{-i})-y_i(0;\mathbf Z_{-i})=1$; hence $$\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N\mathrm E\big[y_i(1;\mathbf D_{-i})-y_i(0;\mathbf D_{-i})\big]=\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N\mathrm E\big[y_i(1;\mathbf Z_{-i})-y_i(0;\mathbf Z_{-i})\big]=1;$$
(e) so the AEED and the average direct effect differ by exactly $\tfrac12$ at every population size, and the difference does not vanish as $N\to\infty$;
(f) reading the labels instead as exposures, no exposure-level outcome schedule exists: for $N=2$ the assignments $(1,0)$ and $(2,0)$ have the same exposure vector $(1,0)$ but $y_1((1,0))=1\ne2=y_1((2,0))$, so there is no function $H_1$ with $y_1(\mathbf z)=H_1(g_1(\mathbf z),g_2(\mathbf z))$ for all $\mathbf z\in\mathcal Z$;
(g) the estimator $\hat\tau(1,0)=\hat{\overline y}(1)-\hat{\overline y}(0)$ of the supplement equals $\frac{3}{2N}\sum_{i=1}^NZ_i$, with $\mathrm E[\hat\tau(1,0)]=\tfrac32$ and $\mathrm{Var}[\hat\tau(1,0)]=\frac{3}{2N}$, so $\Pr\big[|\hat\tau(1,0)-\tfrac32|\ge\epsilon\big]\le\frac{3}{2N\epsilon^2}$ for every $\epsilon>0$: the estimator concentrates on $\tfrac32$, not on the average direct effect $1$.

Let $N=2$, $\mathcal Z=\{0,1\}^2$, $\Pr[\mathbf Z=\mathbf z]=\tfrac14$ for each $\mathbf z\in\mathcal Z$, let the two units be adjacent (adjacency matrix $\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$, so $\theta_1\mathbf z=z_2$ and $\theta_2\mathbf z=z_1$), let $g_i$ be the four-category mapping of Equation (3), and let $y_i(\mathbf z)=z_i$. Then:
(a) $g_1(\mathbf z)=z_1+2z_2$ and $g_2(\mathbf z)=z_2+2z_1$; the exposure vectors of the assignments $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)$ are $(0,0),(2,1),(1,2),(3,3)$ respectively, so $g(\mathcal Z)=\{(0,0),(2,1),(1,2),(3,3)\}$;
(b) $\pi_i(e)=\tfrac14$ for $i\in\{1,2\}$ and every $e\in\{0,1,2,3\}$, so Condition 4.1 holds for $d=1$ and $d'=0$; $\overline y_i(1)=1$ and $\overline y_i(0)=0$, so the AEED $\tau(1,0)=1$ is well-defined; and $y_i$ is constant on every exposure class of unit $i$ over all of $\mathcal Z$, so Condition 5.2 (SUTVA), hence also Condition 5.1 (NURVA), holds;
(c) for no assignment $\mathbf z\in\mathcal Z$ do both hybrid exposure vectors $(1,g_2(\mathbf z))$ and $(0,g_2(\mathbf z))$ belong to $g(\mathcal Z)$, and likewise for $(g_1(\mathbf z),1)$ and $(g_1(\mathbf z),0)$: read with exposure vectors as arguments, at least one of the two terms $y_i(d;\mathbf D_{-i})$, $y_i(d';\mathbf D_{-i})$ of the printed ‘average direct effect’ has no value on every draw;
(d) read literally, with the vectors $(1,D_2)$ and $(0,D_2)$ substituted into the raw potential outcome function $y_1:\mathcal Z\to\mathbb R$ of Equation (1), both vectors lie in $\mathcal Z=\{0,1\}^2$ only when $D_2\in\{0,1\}$, that is, for $\mathbf z\in\{(0,0),(0,1)\}$; with probability $\tfrac12$ ($D_2\in\{2,3\}$) neither lies in the domain of $y_1$; symmetrically for unit 2. So the displayed expectation $\mathrm E[y_i(1;\mathbf D_{-i})-y_i(0;\mathbf D_{-i})]$ is not defined, although positivity and SUTVA hold and the AEED exists;
(e) the same holds at every even population size $N=2m$ for $m$ independent copies of this pair (each exposure depends only on the two assignments of its own pair, and no outcome depends on another unit's assignment).

Counterexample for individualistic exposures (so the defect of P061-T1 and P061-T2 is absent). Fix an integer $m\ge2$. Let there be $K=m^8$ disjoint pairs of units, $N=2m^8$, and put $a=\rho=1/m$. Let $\mathcal Z=\{0,1\}^N$ and $g_i(\mathbf z)=z_i$ (so $\mathbf D=\mathbf Z$). Different pairs are assigned independently; within each pair the assignment $(x,y)\in\{0,1\}^2$ has probability $\frac{1+\rho}{4}$ if $x=y$ and $\frac{1-\rho}{4}$ if $x\ne y$. For unit $i$ with partner $j$ let $y_i(\mathbf z)=1+a\,z_j$ for all $\mathbf z\in\mathcal Z$. Write $T_N=\tau(1,0)$ for the AEED, $A_N=\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N\mathrm E[y_i(1;\mathbf Z_{-i})-y_i(0;\mathbf Z_{-i})]$ for the average direct effect, and $\hat\tau=\hat{\overline y}(1)-\hat{\overline y}(0)$ for the Horvitz–Thompson contrast of the supplement. Then for every $m\ge2$:
(a) every assignment in $\mathcal Z$ has positive probability; $\pi_i(1)=\pi_i(0)=\tfrac12$ for every $i$; all outcomes lie in $[1,\tfrac32]$; each unit's assignment is independent of the assignments of all units outside its pair and each unit's outcome depends on exactly one other unit's assignment (the assignment-dependence graph and the interference graph have maximum degree 1); the covariance of the two assignments of a pair is $\rho/4$;
(b) $A_N=0$;
(c) $\overline y_i(1)=1+a\frac{1+\rho}{2}$ and $\overline y_i(0)=1+a\frac{1-\rho}{2}$, so $T_N=a\rho=1/m^2$ and $\delta_N:=T_N-A_N=1/m^2\to0$;
(d) $\mathrm E[\hat\tau]=T_N$ and $s_N^2:=\mathrm{Var}[\hat\tau]=\dfrac{V_m}{4m^8}$, where $V_m=8(1+\rho)(1+a)+(6+2\rho)a^2-4a^2\rho^2=8+\dfrac{16}{m}+\dfrac{14}{m^2}+\dfrac{2}{m^3}-\dfrac{4}{m^4}$ and $8\le V_m<20$; hence $Ns_N^2=V_m/2\to4$;
(e) $\Big(\dfrac{\delta_N}{s_N}\Big)^2=\dfrac{4m^4}{V_m}\ge\dfrac{m^4}{5}\to\infty$: the two targets converge to each other while their distance in standard-error units diverges ($\delta_N$ is of order $N^{-1/4}$ and $s_N$ of order $N^{-1/2}$);
(f) for the interval $C_m=[\hat\tau-5s_N,\ \hat\tau+5s_N]$: $\Pr[T_N\in C_m]\ge\tfrac{24}{25}$ for every $m\ge2$, and for every $m\ge5$, $\Pr[A_N\in C_m]\le\dfrac{4s_N^2}{\delta_N^2}=\dfrac{V_m}{m^4}\le\dfrac{20}{m^4}\to0$.
So an interval that is valid for the AEED at the exact standard error covers the average direct effect with probability tending to zero, under dependence and interference of degree one, bounded outcomes, exposure probabilities $\tfrac12$ and a variance of exact order $N^{-1}$.
